.TH TAIL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
tail \- output the last part of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-n
.RI [ + ] lines ]
.RB [ \-c
.RI [ + ] bytes ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tail
writes the last 10 lines of each
.I file
to standard output. With no file, or for a file named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input. With more than one file, each is headed by
.BI "==> " name " <=="
(standard input is named
.BR "standard input" ),
with a blank line before every heading but the first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Follows: once the files are shown, waits, and shows what is added to them,
until it's stopped (^C, or
.BR kill ).
Everything is shown the moment it comes: files on a disk or in
.B /tmp
are watched (inotify: a disk reports each change made on it), and pipes
and device files are waited on with
.BR poll ,
all together. A file rewritten rather than
added to (a disk file grown shorter, or a device whose state changed, as a
redstone card's) is shown again from its start, after
.BI "tail: " file ": file truncated"
on standard error. A heading names the file when the text is another
file's than the last shown. Standard input isn't followed. A device file is
read only as far as it has something now, so one that never ends (a motion
sensor's: an event a line) shows the last of the events waiting, then each
as it comes.
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
The last
.I lines
lines.
.RI + lines
starts at line
.I lines
instead (1 is the first), and goes on to the end.
.TP
.BI \-c " bytes"
The last
.I bytes
bytes;
.RI + bytes
starts at that byte and goes on to the end.
.TP
.BI \- number
The same as
.B \-n
.IR number .
.PP
The number may be joined to its option
.RB ( \-n5 )
or follow it as the next argument. A later
.B \-n
undoes an earlier
.BR \-c .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a number or an option is invalid, or a file can't be
opened (the other files are still shown).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tail \-n 3 /init.lua
tail \-n +2 list.txt
ps | tail \-c 100
tail \-f /dev/redstone0
tail \-f /dev/motion0
.fi
.SH NOTES
What another program writes to a file on a disk shows when it closes the
file (a disk's files take writes at close), so
.B tail \-f
sees a line once its writer has closed the file;
.B >>
in the shell closes it at once.
GNU
.B tail
reads a device's file to its end first, so on one that never ends (a
sensor's events) it shows nothing; with
.BR \-f ,
this one reads only what the device has now, then follows it.
A change made to a disk's file outside the computer (on the disk's host)
isn't seen until the next change made inside it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR grep (1)
